    <body>
      <sec id="article-25199.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Mitral stenosis is a valvular disorder defined by narrowing of the mitral valve orifice, leading to impaired left ventricular filling, elevated left atrial pressure, and progressive pulmonary congestion. Most cases result from rheumatic heart disease, although degenerative and congenital causes also occur. Clinical manifestations include exertional dyspnea, fatigue, palpitations, and complications such as atrial fibrillation and thromboembolism. This course provides clinicians with a comprehensive review of epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and echocardiographic evaluation, with emphasis on disease severity assessment and hemodynamic impact. Evidence-based management strategies are discussed, including pharmacologic therapy, anticoagulation, and indications for percutaneous or surgical intervention. Collaboration within an interprofessional team, including cardiology, imaging specialists, and surgical services, enhances diagnostic accuracy, optimizes timing of intervention, and improves overall patient outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based clinical guidelines to diagnose and manage mitral stenosis across the spectrum of disease severity, integrating patient-specific factors such as age, comorbidities, pregnancy status, and risk of thromboembolism into clinical decision-making.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate the indications, contraindications, and management strategies for anticoagulation therapy, particularly in patients with atrial fibrillation, prior embolic events, or left atrial thrombus.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Screen high-risk populations, including individuals with a history of rheumatic fever or endemic exposure, using appropriate clinical evaluation and echocardiographic techniques for early detection.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the&#x000a0;interprofessional healthcare team, including cardiologists, cardiothoracic surgeons, anesthesiologists, and allied health professionals, to enhance care coordination, optimize management decisions, and improve clinical outcomes for patients diagnosed with mitral stenosis.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=25199&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=25199">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Mitral stenosis is a progressive valvular disorder characterized by obstruction of left ventricular inflow due to narrowing of the mitral valve orifice. The condition most commonly results from chronic rheumatic heart disease, which leads to commissural fusion, leaflet thickening, and subvalvular fibrosis, ultimately impairing normal valve opening during diastole. Although the global epidemiology of mitral stenosis has evolved with declining rates of rheumatic fever in many high-income countries, rheumatic disease remains a leading cause worldwide. In developed regions, the condition is increasingly associated with degenerative calcific disease of the mitral annulus and leaflets, particularly among older adults and patients with chronic kidney disease or metabolic disorders.</p>
        <p>The hemodynamic consequences of mitral stenosis arise from impaired diastolic filling of the left ventricle, leading to elevated left atrial pressure, pulmonary venous congestion, and progressive pulmonary hypertension. Over time, these changes may result in atrial enlargement, atrial fibrillation, right ventricular dysfunction, and reduced cardiac output. Clinical manifestations often develop insidiously and include exertional dyspnea, fatigue, palpitations, and symptoms related to pulmonary congestion or systemic embolization. Because disease progression can be gradual, patients may remain asymptomatic for years before presenting with significant hemodynamic compromise or complications.</p>
        <p>Advances in diagnostic imaging, particularly transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiography, have improved the ability to accurately assess mitral valve anatomy, quantify stenosis severity, and evaluate associated structural and hemodynamic abnormalities. These assessments play a central role in determining disease severity, monitoring progression, and guiding management decisions. Contemporary treatment strategies include medical therapy for symptom control and complication prevention, percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy for suitable valve anatomy, and surgical repair or replacement in advanced or complex cases.</p>
        <p>This review provides a comprehensive overview of mitral stenosis for clinicians&#x000a0;by examining the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, and evidence-based management of the disease, with emphasis on current guideline-directed therapies and procedural interventions. By integrating current literature and clinical recommendations, this activity aims to support clinicians in improving recognition, risk stratification, and management of patients with mitral stenosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Globally, the predominant cause of mitral stenosis remains the chronic sequelae of rheumatic heart disease, especially in low and middle-income countries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r1">[1]</xref> In the United States and other developed nations, mitral stenosis cases are mostly attributable to nonrheumatic causes, particularly degenerative changes with severe mitral annular calcification&#x000a0;that extend onto the valve leaflets. Uncommon causes of mitral stenosis are calcification of the mitral valve leaflets and congenital heart disease. Other causes of mitral stenosis include infective endocarditis, endomyocardial fibroelastosis, malignant carcinoid syndrome, systemic lupus erythematosus,&#x000a0;deposition of urate crystals in advanced tophaceous gout, Whipple disease, Fabry disease and other metabolic storage diseases, rheumatoid arthritis, and iatrogenic as a complication of transcatheter mitral valve repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The prevalence of rheumatic disease in developed countries is declining, with an estimated incidence of 1 in 100,000. The prevalence is higher in developing nations than in the United States. In Africa, for example, the prevalence is 35 cases per 100,000.&#x000a0;Rheumatic mitral stenosis is more&#x000a0;common in women, as approximately 80% of mitral stenosis patients in the world are women. The onset of symptoms in rheumatic mitral disease typically occurs between the third and fourth decades of life, whereas degenerative mitral stenosis typically occurs later in life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The mitral valve is a bileaflet valve positioned between the left atrium and left ventricle. Under normal physiologic conditions, the mitral valve opens during the left ventricular diastole to allow blood to flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle. The pressure in the left atrium and the left ventricle during diastole is equal. The left ventricle gets filled with blood during early ventricular diastole. Only&#x000a0;a small amount of blood remains in the left atrium. This small amount of blood fills the left ventricle with the contraction&#x000a0;of the left atrium (the "atrial kick") during late ventricular diastole.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Rheumatic Mitral Stenosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>This entity evolves insidiously, with a prolonged asymptomatic interval between the initial rheumatic episode and the onset of clinically significant stenosis. In resource-limited settings, this latent phase tends to be shorter, likely due to recurrent episodes of rheumatic carditis. Repeated inflammatory insults accelerate valvular damage, leading to fibrotic thickening and calcification of the leaflets, fusion of the commissures, and shortening or thickening of the chordae tendineae. These pathological changes progressively narrow the mitral orifice.</p>
        <p>Results from recent studies suggest that the physiologic mitral valve area averages around 10 cm&#x000b2; during mitral valve opening, although the current consensus of &#x0201c;normal&#x0201d; mitral annular area is still around 4 to 6 cm&#x000b2;.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r10">[10]</xref> This large surface area allows unobstructed flow from the left atrium to the left ventricle with a negligible pressure gradient.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r11">[11]</xref> In mitral stenosis, narrowing of the orifice elevates left atrial pressure and volume, which is transmitted retrogradely to the pulmonary venous and capillary circulation. This increased hydrostatic pressure may result in pulmonary edema and exertional dyspnea. In severe cases, hemoptysis can result from the formation of bronchopulmonary venous collaterals and the rupture of engorged bronchial veins under high pressure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Nonrheumatic Mitral Stenosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>In developed nations, nonrheumatic mitral stenosis is most often secondary to advanced mitral annular calcification. This condition involves dense calcium deposition along the annulus, extending into the bases of the valve leaflets, thereby reducing annular flexibility and restricting leaflet motion. Unlike the rheumatic process, commissural fusion is absent, but the calcific encroachment can significantly impair valvular opening, leading to hemodynamic consequences similar to those seen in rheumatic disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Mitral valve areas less than 2 cm&#x000b2; cause an impediment to the blood flow from the left atrium into the&#x000a0;left ventricle. This creates a pressure gradient across the mitral valve. As the gradient across the mitral valve increases, the left ventricle requires the atrial kick to fill.</p>
        <p>Mitral stenosis causes an increase in left atrial pressure. The normal left ventricular diastolic pressure is 5 mm Hg. A pressure gradient across the mitral valve of 20 mm Hg due to severe mitral stenosis will cause a left atrial pressure of about 25 mm Hg. This left atrial pressure is transmitted to the pulmonary vasculature, resulting in pulmonary hypertension. As left atrial pressure remains elevated, the left atrium will enlarge. An enlarged left atrium or elevated mean pressure gradient across the mitral valve increases the likelihood of atrial fibrillation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>If atrial fibrillation develops, the atrial kick is lost. Thus, the left ventricular filling depends on the atrial kick in severe mitral stenosis. With the loss of the atrial kick, there is a decrease in cardiac output and sudden development of congestive heart failure. Mitral stenosis progresses slowly from initial signs of mitral stenosis to New York Heart Association (NYHA) functional class II symptoms to atrial fibrillation to NYHA functional class III or IV symptoms.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Mitral stenosis typically presents insidiously, often becoming clinically apparent 10 to 20 years after an episode of rheumatic fever. Symptoms primarily reflect progressive obstruction to left ventricular inflow and the resulting elevation in left atrial and pulmonary venous pressures. The most common presenting symptoms are exertional dyspnea, orthopnea, and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea, which arise from pulmonary venous congestion. Patients may also report fatigue and reduced exercise tolerance due to decreased cardiac output.</p>
        <p>Palpitations are common and often related to the development of atrial fibrillation secondary to&#x000a0;chronic left atrial enlargement. Additional manifestations may include chest discomfort, hemoptysis from pulmonary venous hypertension and bronchial vein rupture, and systemic thromboembolism due to left atrial stasis. As pulmonary hypertension develops and right ventricular function declines, patients may develop peripheral edema, ascites, and hepatomegaly. Physiologic states that increase cardiac output, such as pregnancy, fever, anemia, or physical exertion, may exacerbate symptoms because the shortened diastolic filling period further increases the transmitral gradient.</p>
        <p>The physical examination findings of mitral stenosis are characteristic and reflect altered valvular mechanics and progressive pulmonary hypertension. On cardiac auscultation, the first heart sound (S1) is typically loud and sometimes palpable due to the forceful closure of the stenotic mitral valve leaflets. A loud pulmonic component of the second heart sound (accentuated P2) may be present when pulmonary hypertension develops.</p>
        <p>An opening snap occurring shortly after the A2 component of the second heart sound is a classic finding and represents the abrupt tensing of the stenotic mitral valve as it opens under elevated left atrial pressure. The interval between A2 and the opening snap shortens as stenosis becomes more severe. Following the opening snap, a low-pitched, middiastolic rumbling murmur with presystolic accentuation is typically heard best with the bell of the stethoscope at the cardiac apex, particularly when the patient is positioned in the left lateral decubitus position or after isometric exercise.</p>
        <p>Presystolic accentuation may disappear in the presence of atrial fibrillation. Additional findings may include a tapping apical impulse, reflecting a palpable S1, and signs of right ventricular pressure overload, such as a left parasternal heave. In advanced disease, physical examination may reveal features of right-sided heart failure, including jugular venous distension, hepatomegaly, ascites, and peripheral edema, as well as evidence of long-standing pulmonary hypertension.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Evaluation of mitral stenosis involves a combination of clinical assessment, noninvasive imaging, and, when necessary, invasive hemodynamic studies. The condition is classified&#x000a0;by severity based on valve area, transmitral gradient, and pulmonary pressures. Initial evaluation typically includes electrocardiography (ECG), chest radiography, transthoracic echocardiography (TTE), and transesophageal echocardiography (TEE), with additional testing such as exercise echocardiography or cardiac catheterization performed when further diagnostic clarification or procedural planning is required.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>ECG frequently demonstrates abnormalities related to chronic left atrial pressure overload. Characteristic findings include P-wave changes consistent with left atrial enlargement, often described as broad or notched P waves. As the disease progresses, atrial arrhythmias, most commonly atrial fibrillation, are frequently detected due to progressive atrial dilation and fibrosis. Evidence of right axis deviation and right ventricular hypertrophy may develop in advanced disease and suggest the presence of significant pulmonary hypertension.</p>
        <p>Chest radiography may provide supportive diagnostic information, particularly in moderate-to-advanced disease. In the early stages of mitral stenosis, the cardiac silhouette may appear normal. However, subtle findings such as straightening of the left heart border, prominence of the main pulmonary arteries, dilation of the upper pulmonary veins, and posterior displacement of the esophagus by an enlarged left atrium may be observed. As the disease progresses, chest radiographs may demonstrate left atrial enlargement, enlargement of the pulmonary arteries and veins, and, eventually, enlargement of additional cardiac chambers. Findings of pulmonary venous congestion or interstitial edema may also be present in symptomatic patients with elevated pulmonary venous pressures.</p>
        <p>Echocardiography remains the cornerstone of diagnosis and severity assessment in mitral stenosis.&#x000a0;TTE allows direct measurement of the mitral valve area, assessment of the mean transmitral gradient, and estimation of pulmonary artery pressures using Doppler techniques. Echocardiography also defines the etiology and morphology of the mitral valve apparatus, including leaflet mobility, leaflet thickening, calcification, commissural fusion, and involvement of the subvalvular apparatus.</p>
        <p>Morphologic scoring systems help determine suitability for percutaneous intervention. The Padial score evaluates leaflet thickening, commissural calcification, and subvalvular disease. Similarly, the Wilkins score grades&#x000a0;4 components: leaflet mobility, leaflet thickness, leaflet calcification, and subvalvular thickening on a scale of 1 to 4 based on severity. The total score ranges from 4 to 16.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;The grading is as follows:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grade 1</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mobility
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Highly mobile valve with only leaflet tips restricted</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Leaflet near normal in thickness (4&#x02013;5 mm)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Calcification
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A single area of increased echo brightness</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Subvalvular thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Minimal thickening just below the mitral leaflets</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grade 2</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mobility
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Leaflet mid to base portions have normal mobility</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mid leaflets normal, considerable thickening of margins (5&#x02013;8 mm)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Calcification
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Scattered areas of brightness confirmed to leaflet margins</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Subvalvular thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Thickening of chordal structures extending to one of the chordal lengths</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grade 3</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mobility
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Valve continues to move forward in diastole, mainly from the base</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Thickening extending through the entire leaflet (5&#x02013;8 mm)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Calcification
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Brightness extending into the mid portions of the leaflets</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Subvalvular thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Thickening extended to the distal third of the chords</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Grade</bold>
<bold>4</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Mobility
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>No or minimal forward movement of the leaflets in diastole</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Considerable thickening of all leaflet tissue (more than 8&#x02013;10 mm)</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Calcification
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Extensive brightness throughout much of the leaflet tissue</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Subvalvular thickening
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Extensive thickening and shortening of all chordal structures extending down to the papillary muscles.&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Patients with Wilkins scores less than 8, Padial scores less than 10, and less-than-moderate mitral regurgitation tend to have better outcomes following percutaneous balloon mitral valvotomy (PBMV). Patients with a Wilkins score greater than 12 are considered poor PBMV candidates, and surgery is preferred.&#x000a0;Additional imaging may further refine the evaluation. TEE&#x000a0;is particularly valuable when transthoracic imaging is limited or when assessing for left atrial thrombus before percutaneous mitral balloon valvotomy. Exercise echocardiography may be performed on an upright treadmill or a supine bicycle, with Doppler assessment of transmitral and tricuspid valve velocities. This test evaluates changes in transmitral gradient and pulmonary artery systolic pressure during exertion, helping clarify symptom&#x02013;severity discrepancies and identify patients who develop significant hemodynamic compromise with activity.</p>
        <p>Cardiac catheterization remains the definitive invasive method for hemodynamic assessment, but is now typically reserved for selected circumstances. The procedure directly measures left atrial pressure, pulmonary artery pressures, and the transmitral pressure gradient, allowing calculation of the mitral valve area using the Gorlin formula. Current guidelines recommend cardiac catheterization when noninvasive tests are inconclusive or when there is discordance between clinical findings and echocardiographic assessment of disease severity (Class I, Level of Evidence C).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;(See&#x000a0;<bold>Table.</bold>&#x000a0;Classification of Severity of Mitral Stenosis.) Additionally, coronary angiography is often performed in patients undergoing surgical intervention or percutaneous procedures who have risk factors for coronary artery disease.</p>
