          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of mitral stenosis focuses on symptom control, prevention of complications, and, when indicated, mechanical relief of the fixed obstruction. Because the fundamental pathology is structural narrowing of the mitral valve orifice, no medical therapy can reverse or directly relieve the obstruction. Consequently, treatment strategies include medical therapy for symptom management and complication prevention, PMBV, and surgical intervention for definitive mechanical correction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Medical therapy plays an important role in stabilization and addressing the disease's hemodynamic consequences. In patients who remain in normal sinus rhythm, pharmacologic therapy is primarily directed at reducing pulmonary congestion and improving exercise tolerance. Diuretics are commonly used to relieve pulmonary venous congestion and peripheral edema. Beta-blockers or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers may be used to control heart rate and prolong diastolic filling time, thereby reducing the transmitral gradient during exertion. Patients should also receive appropriate secondary prevention for rheumatic fever, typically with benzathine penicillin prophylaxis, which remains an essential strategy for preventing recurrent streptococcal infections and further rheumatic valve damage.</p>
        <p>Atrial fibrillation is a common complication of mitral stenosis due to chronic left atrial enlargement and fibrosis, and often contributes significantly to symptom burden. Management focuses on rate control, rhythm control when feasible, and prevention of thromboembolism. Initial therapy typically includes atrioventricular nodal blocking agents such as beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, or digitalis to control ventricular rate.</p>
        <p>Hemodynamically unstable individuals require urgent direct current cardioversion. In those who are stable, restoration of sinus rhythm may be considered to improve exercise capacity and quality of life. However, long-term rhythm maintenance may be challenging in the presence of marked atrial enlargement.</p>
        <p>Because mitral stenosis carries a high risk of systemic embolization, anticoagulation is strongly recommended for patients with mitral stenosis and atrial fibrillation (paroxysmal, persistent, or permanent), a history of thromboembolic events, or a documented left atrial thrombus. Warfarin remains the anticoagulant of choice, with a target international normalized ratio of approximately 2.5. Antiplatelet therapy alone, such as aspirin, has not been shown to reduce thromboembolic risk in this population adequately.</p>
        <p>Preventive measures also play a role in management. Infective endocarditis prophylaxis is recommended only for high-risk individuals undergoing dental procedures that involve manipulation of gingival tissue or perforation of the oral mucosa. Those at high-risk include patients with prosthetic heart valves, prosthetic material used for valve repair, or a prior history of infective endocarditis. Routine prophylaxis is not recommended for most patients with native mitral stenosis.</p>
        <p>When symptoms progress or hemodynamic severity increases, mechanical intervention to relieve the obstruction becomes necessary. PMBV,&#x000a0;also referred to as percutaneous mitral balloon commissurotomy,&#x000a0;is the preferred treatment for appropriately selected patients. The procedure enlarges the mitral valve orifice by splitting fused commissures, thereby increasing valve area and reducing the transmitral pressure gradient. PMBV is indicated in symptomatic persons with moderate to severe mitral stenosis (NYHA class greater than II) and in selected asymptomatic individuals with significant pulmonary hypertension, provided that valve morphology is favorable, and there is no left atrial thrombus or moderate to severe mitral regurgitation. Outcomes with PMBV are generally favorable, and short- and long-term results are comparable to those achieved with surgical commissurotomy in appropriately selected patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Surgical intervention is recommended for patients with symptomatic moderate or severe mitral stenosis who are not candidates for PMBV, typically due to unfavorable valve morphology, significant calcification, left atrial thrombus, or associated moderate-to-severe mitral regurgitation. In these cases, mitral valve replacement is most commonly performed, although surgical commissurotomy may be considered in selected circumstances. Surgical treatment remains a highly effective option for relieving obstruction and improving functional status when percutaneous therapy is contraindicated or unsuccessful (Class I, Level of Evidence B).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r12">[12]</xref> Advances in minimally invasive and robotic mitral valve surgery have also expanded operative options in appropriately selected patients.</p>
        <p>Overall, optimal management of mitral stenosis requires careful integration of medical therapy, rhythm management, anticoagulation, and timely mechanical intervention, guided by symptom status, hemodynamic severity, and valve morphology. Early recognition of disease progression and appropriate referral for percutaneous or surgical therapy are critical to preventing irreversible pulmonary hypertension, right heart failure, and other long-term complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnoses for mitral stenosis include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Left atrial myxoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocarditis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of mitral stenosis depends on the severity of the obstruction, the development of complications such as atrial fibrillation and pulmonary hypertension, and the timing of definitive intervention. The disease often follows a prolonged latent phase. Mitral valve stenosis may remain asymptomatic for many years, particularly when it develops as a consequence of rheumatic fever, and it may take several decades after the initial rheumatic insult for clinically significant stenosis to appear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r25">[25]</xref> During this period, progressive commissural fusion, leaflet thickening, and subvalvular fibrosis gradually reduce the mitral valve area.</p>
        <p>Once symptoms emerge, most commonly exertional dyspnea, the course of the disease often accelerates. Historically, approximately 80% of patients did not survive beyond 10 years after symptom onset, particularly when the disease resulted from rheumatic pathology. The development of pulmonary hypertension significantly worsens prognosis, with average survival decreasing to approximately&#x000a0;3 years once severe pulmonary hypertension develops. Advanced disease is frequently accompanied by right-sided heart failure, which further contributes to morbidity and mortality.</p>
        <p>The onset of atrial fibrillation represents another major turning point in disease progression. Chronic left atrial pressure overload leads to atrial enlargement and electrical remodeling, predisposing patients to atrial fibrillation. Loss of coordinated atrial contraction reduces diastolic ventricular filling and cardiac output, often precipitating clinical deterioration. In addition, atrial fibrillation increases the risk of systemic thromboembolism, including ischemic stroke, due to stasis within the enlarged left atrium. Stroke, persistent atrial fibrillation, and progressive heart failure remain important contributors to morbidity in patients with advanced mitral stenosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Prognosis has improved substantially with advances in diagnostic imaging and interventional therapies. Prior to the development of modern cardiac surgery, outcomes for patients with mitral stenosis were generally poor. In the current era, PMBV&#x000a0;and surgical mitral valve replacement provide effective relief of obstruction and significantly improve survival. Patients undergoing mitral valve replacement now demonstrate approximately 80% survival at 10 years, with outcomes generally superior to those historically reported with surgical commissurotomy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r27">[27]</xref> Nevertheless, prognosis remains less favorable in patients who present late in the disease course, particularly those with established pulmonary hypertension or right ventricular dysfunction.</p>
        <p>Congenital mitral stenosis represents a distinct clinical entity with variable outcomes. The prognosis in children depends largely on the severity of the anatomic abnormality and the presence of associated congenital heart defects, and many patients require lifelong cardiologic follow-up and periodic evaluation for additional structural abnormalities or progressive obstruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r28">[28]</xref> Overall, early recognition of disease progression, aggressive management of complications such as atrial fibrillation and thromboembolism, and timely referral for definitive intervention remain critical determinants of long-term outcomes in patients with mitral stenosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of mitral stenosis include:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Heart failure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stroke</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to thrive</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary hypertension</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocarditis</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Cardiac surgery consultations should be obtained in cases of severe mitral stenosis to evaluate the patient for mitral valve replacement if the patient is ineligible for the PMBV procedure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence and patient education play a critical role in reducing the progression and complications associated with mitral stenosis. Because the most common global cause of mitral stenosis remains rheumatic heart disease, prevention strategies focus primarily on early recognition and treatment of group A streptococcal pharyngitis and prevention of recurrent rheumatic fever. Prompt antibiotic therapy for streptococcal infections and secondary prophylaxis with long-acting benzathine penicillin in patients with a history of rheumatic fever can significantly reduce recurrent inflammatory injury to the mitral valve and limit disease progression. Public health measures such as improved access to primary care, early screening in endemic regions, and education regarding the importance of treating sore throat infections remain essential components of global disease prevention.</p>
        <p>Patient education is also essential for individuals diagnosed with mitral stenosis. Patients should be counseled to recognize symptoms indicating disease progression, including worsening exertional dyspnea, orthopnea, palpitations, fatigue, and lower-extremity swelling. All patients with mitral valve stenosis, regardless of severity, may require medications to prevent complications like strokes, hypertension, and heart failure; these are usually necessary for the individual's lifespan, and the importance of medication adherence must be stressed to these patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25199.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Education regarding the risk of atrial fibrillation and thromboembolism is important, particularly for patients who require long-term anticoagulation therapy. These patients should understand the need for medication adherence, regular monitoring of the International normalized ratio when using warfarin, and recognition of bleeding complications. Patients should also be informed about lifestyle modifications that reduce symptom burden, including maintaining appropriate physical activity within their tolerance, avoiding excessive sodium intake, which may worsen congestion, and seeking prompt medical evaluation for infections, arrhythmias, or worsening symptoms.</p>
        <p>In addition, patients should be educated about the importance of regular cardiology follow-up, including periodic echocardiographic monitoring to assess valve severity and detect complications such as pulmonary hypertension or worsening valve obstruction. Individuals undergoing percutaneous or surgical intervention should receive counseling regarding postoperative care, anticoagulation when indicated, and the potential need for future valve-related procedures. Education regarding infective endocarditis prevention is also important, particularly for high-risk individuals with prosthetic valves or prior endocarditis who require antibiotic prophylaxis before certain dental procedures. Through comprehensive patient education and preventive strategies, clinicians can help reduce complications, delay disease progression, and improve long-term outcomes in patients with mitral stenosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Pregnancy during mitral stenosis will increase the patient&#x02019;s symptoms by&#x000a0;1 NYHA class. Medical therapy is attempted first to improve symptoms. If symptoms do not improve with medical treatment, refer the patient for PMBV.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Optimal care of patients with mitral stenosis requires a well-coordinated interprofessional approach to reduce morbidity, prevent complications, and improve quality of life. Cardiologists, cardiothoracic surgeons, cardiac anesthesiologists, advanced practice practitioners, nurses, pharmacists, echocardiographers, and rehabilitation specialists all contribute essential expertise. Early and accurate diagnosis is paramount and relies on precise echocardiographic assessment, auscultatory recognition of classic murmurs, and functional evaluation of symptom severity.</p>
        <p>Evidence-based decision-making is crucial for determining the timing and modality of intervention, whether percutaneous balloon mitral valvotomy, surgical repair, or valve replacement. Nursing staff play a pivotal role in monitoring for decompensation and educating patients on anticoagulation, fluid management, and symptom tracking. Pharmacists optimize medical therapy, ensuring effective rate control, diuresis, and anticoagulation, while minimizing drug interactions and adverse effects. Integration of cardiac rehabilitation and patient education into the care plan fosters adherence and empowers patients to participate actively in their management.</p>
        <p>A strategic and ethical framework underpins the interprofessional care of mitral stenosis. Clear delineation of roles ensures that each team member&#x02019;s expertise is leveraged effectively; surgeons and interventionalists manage procedural planning and execution, cardiologists oversee longitudinal medical optimization, and nurses ensure continuity of care across inpatient and outpatient settings. Seamless communication channels, including structured case conferences and shared electronic medical records, facilitate timely information exchange and coordinated decision-making.</p>
        <p>Ethical principles such as informed consent, patient autonomy, and shared decision-making are integral to ensuring that patients understand the risks, benefits, and alternatives of proposed interventions. Effective care coordination, from diagnosis through intervention and follow-up, reduces procedural delays, minimizes complications, and enhances recovery. By integrating clinical skills, evidence-based strategies, ethical practice, and robust interprofessional communication, healthcare teams can significantly improve outcomes for patients with mitral stenosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25199.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
